WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.647
It's eight in the evening.

00:00:01.647 --> 00:00:04.911
Upstairs and downstairs, light spills from every window.

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:07.252
The family heads out.

00:00:07.252 --> 00:00:09.247
The front door closes behind them.

00:00:09.247 --> 00:00:10.612
All eight lights stay on.

00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:16.864
Eight sixty-watt lamps, left on for eight hours, use three point eight four kilowatt-hours.

00:00:16.864 --> 00:00:22.091
At twenty-five euro cents per kilowatt-hour, that's ninety-six cents to light an empty house.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.483
Let's replay that night.

00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:26.861
The last person out switches off the lights.

00:00:26.861 --> 00:00:30.104
Over the same eight hours, those lamps add zero to the bill.

00:00:31.042 --> 00:00:32.375
Someone comes home.

00:00:32.375 --> 00:00:33.730
One room lights up.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.522
Your API can work the same way.

00:00:36.458 --> 00:00:39.648
With SlimFaas, an idle API can scale to zero.

00:00:39.648 --> 00:00:43.488
The next HTTP call wakes a worker and waits for its answer.

00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:46.113
Watch this real deployment.

00:00:46.113 --> 00:00:48.427
Thirty calculations arrived together.

00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:50.027
Four workers shared the load.

00:00:50.958 --> 00:00:52.292
All thirty finished.

00:00:52.292 --> 00:00:55.641
After the idle timeout, the worker count returned to zero.

00:00:56.542 --> 00:01:02.259
At Devoxx Belgium, one team reported cutting its infrastructure costs by more than sixty percent.

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:07.177
Try SlimFaas locally with one of your own HTTP endpoints.
